onfirmed) and does not appear in the open-question evaluation below.\n\n## Component Mappings\n\n- **rhods-operator → \"OpenShift AI Productization\"**: rhods-operator is not a canonical component in `.context/rhai-components.txt`; \"OpenShift AI Productization\" is the closest parent-level component for the RHOAI platform operator. Applies to the investigation, manager, cloudmanager, and CI lint epics.\n- **Contribution guideline documentation → \"Documentation\"**: canonical match for the docs-authoring work product.\n- rhods-operator **is in the platform** (architecture context file exists: `architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`, confirming controller-runtime/logr usage, the cloudmanager binary, and the shared reconciler pattern) — no onboarding chain is needed.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Resolve structured log field convention scope (fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind) | Investigation | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize reconciliation error logs across the rhods-operator manager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Standardize reconciliation error logs in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | CI lint rule enforcing structured log fields in reconciliation error paths | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E005 | Document and publish the structured log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigation: log field convention scope\"] --> E005[\"E005 Docs: convention guideline + publication\"]\n    E002[\"E002 Implementation: manager binary log standardization\"] --> E005\n    E003[\"E003 Implementation: cloudmanager binary log standardization\"] --> E005\n    E004[\"E004 Implementation: CI lint rule\"] --> E005\n```\n\nRoots (parallel start): E001, E002, E003, E004. Critical path length: 2 (e.g., E002 → E005).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E005 | Step 5 Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The convention field-set decision (open question: fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind, e.g. reconciliation attempt count, error category) determines the contribution guideline's content. True gate: `rewrite` — the doc must specify additional fields and their semantics if the convention is extended. Recorded via `gated_by` on E005. |\n| E002 → E005 | Step 5 Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementations) | Docs describe what was built; must follow manager implementation. |\n| E003 → E005 | Step 5 Rule 11 | Docs must follow cloudmanager implementation. |\n| E004 → E005 | Step 5 Rule 11 | The guideline references the CI enforcement mechanism; docs follow it. |\n| No E002 ↔ E003 edge | Step 5 Rule 15 (same repo, different areas → parallel) | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries with disjoint controllers; merge conflicts are a coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n| No E004 → E002/E003 edge | Step 5 Rule 15 / Rule 10 analog | The lint rule is developed in parallel; compliance is enforced as an acceptance criterion gate on the implementation epics (\"lint passes once available\"), never by a DAG dependency. |\n| No E001 → E002/E003/E004 edge | Step 3 decision rule | The P0 HLRs fix the required fields (name, namespace, resourceKind); the open question concerns optional extensions only. The implementation epics do the same work regardless of the outcome — any adopted extra fields would be additive follow-up scope, not a change to what these epics build. Hence E001 gates only E005. |\n\n### Open-question evaluation (Step 3)\n\n| Open question | Changes which epics exist / what they do? | Handling |\n|---------------|-------------------------------------------|----------|\n| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (attempt count, error category)? | Yes — changes what the guideline documents (and would add follow-up scope) | Investigation epic E001, gating E005 (`rewrite`) |\n| Which existing log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns? | No — implementation proceeds identically; failure = fix-and-retry | Acceptance criterion on E002/E003 (log consumer regression validation, SRE/Support coordination) |\n| Cloudmanager same release or subsequent release? | No — the epic exists and does the same work either way; only timing changes | E003 is separately scoped so it can follow in a subsequent release; flagged as a health warning for PM |\n| Actual percentage of log calls embedding identity (75% claimed)? | No — the conversion work is identical regardless of the percentage | Acceptance criterion on E002/E003 (grep/static-analysis audit with coverage checklist) |\n\nStrategy prerequisites (\"Pending human review\": architecture review, build onboarding, licence validation, etc.) are process gates on the strategy, not technical unknowns — they do not generate epics and are captured as `human_process_gates = -1` on the implementation epics.\n\n### Epic boundary decisions\n\n- **Manager binary = one epic (E002)**: same component, same team, same repo, one uniform logical change (mechanical conversion to one convention) estimated at ~0.75–1 sprint total. The five controller tiers are not split into separate epics — that would be over-decomposition of a single mechanical pattern.\n- **P1 HLR (resourceKind on DSC/component controllers) stays bundled in E002**: it edits the exact same `log.Error` call sites as the P0 identity-conversion work in those controllers — tightly coupled to the same implementation surface; splitting would duplicate edits and create artificial boundaries (Step 4 bundling rule).\n- **DSCI key-name migration stays bundled in E002**: same controller, same call sites, same PR review cycle as the identity conversion; its distinct risks (single-release transition, query breakage) are captured as acceptance criteria.\n- **Cloudmanager = separate epic (E003)**: distinct binary, disjoint controllers, separately deployable (non-OpenShift clouds only), and a PM open question explicitly contemplates deferring it to a subsequent release.\n- **CI lint rule = separate epic (E004)**: distinct work product (CI/static-analysis tooling vs. Go log edits) with a different review cycle; it is repo-wide and must not be serialized against either binary's implementation.\n- **Docs = separate epic (E005)**: substantive new content (contribution guideline, migration guide, external publication), a different work-product type warranting `docs-authoring` routing. Priority P1 per the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter priority: Major → P1); it does not push priority upstream.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace converted to structured KV pairs | P0 | E002 (manager), E003 (cloudmanager); enforced/measured by E004 |\n| DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | P0 | E002 |\n| Service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 |\n| All 15 action-file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) | P0 | E002 |\n| Cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E003 |\n| Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E002 |\n| DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (queryability enhancement) | P1 | E002 (bundled — same call sites as P0 work) |\n| Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E005 (gated by E001) |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Cloudmanager release phasing (open question)**: E003 is P0 per its HLR, but the strategy's open question (owner: PM / ODH Operator team) may defer it to a subsequent release. The epic is separately scoped to permit this; PM should confirm phasing before planning.\n- **Convention extension risk**: if investigation E001 decides to add fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind, the implementation epics (which implement the HLR-mandated fields only) would need follow-up scope for the additional fields. This is accepted to avoid serializing all P0 work behind a product decision.\n- **Unconfirmed audit figure**: the \"75% of log calls embed identity\" figure is SME-estimated and unvalidated; the audit is an acceptance criterion on E002/E003, and the checklist mitigates missed call sites.\n- **Pending strategy process gates**: architecture review, build onboarding, and licence validation are all \"Pending human review\" — these gate strategy execution (reflected in `human_process_gates = -1`) but are not decomposed into epics.\n- **Log consumer regression risk**: text-based alert rules may break when message strings change; captured as acceptance criteria (dev-cluster validation with the production log consumer stack, SRE/Support coordination) rather than a separate epic, per the strategy's own mitigation.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe only external dependency with uncertain timing is **COO team adoption** (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator log format changes). Per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI neither mandates nor manages external operator dependencies, and the RFE approval comment confirms voluntary adoption — so no Tier 2 epic is modeled for COO work. All value in this strategy (Tier 1) is delivered independently of COO adoption: the rhods-operator log standardization, CI enforcement, and the published convention. E005 includes filing tracking issues in the COO projects as best-effort outreach; adoption timing is outside this decomposition's control.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":52680,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae39cf41832eed50","agent_id":"a1458638327bcdef4","session_id":"91ced742-efea-4af7-ba7b-29299f310824","uuid":"39749f6a-4a7d-40f0-9887-5c6b5b165c7b","timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:53:38.967Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
