{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-b4a4b668-f0c7-40ac-b753-4ff711af212f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ec6fcb0fb155460","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-bd6rkjmq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E002.md","content":"# Standardize reconciliation error logs across the rhods-operator manager binary\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic converts all reconciliation error logs in the rhods-operator manager binary to the structured logging convention: resource name and namespace emitted as structured key-value pairs instead of being embedded in message strings, `resourceKind` added as a structured field to every controller and action-file error log, and the DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller migrated off its non-standard key names. The work spans all five controller tiers of the manager binary — the DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller, the 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI), the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator), the DSCI controller, and the 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) — plus all 15 action files across the four tiers (8 component action files under `components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`, 3 service action files under `services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`, 1 DSC action file under `datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`, and 3 module action files under `modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`).\n\nThe logging infrastructure (controller-runtime logr) already exists, and the framework controllers (DSC and the 8 component controllers) already emit `name` and `namespace` via controller-runtime's request logger at the reconcile entry point; the missing pieces are the field conversions, the `resourceKind` field, and the DSCI key-name migration.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the manager binary's controllers and action files, using grep/static analysis, and track coverage in a checklist (the strategy's 75% embedded-identity figure is an estimate to be confirmed by this audit)\n- For each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), leaving the log message as a stable, identity-free string\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all reconciliation error logs:\n  - Service controllers, module controller (all 8 module handlers), DSCI controller, and all 15 action files — required (P0), because the reconciled kind is not implied by the controller name for these\n  - DSC and the 8 component controllers — queryability enhancement (P1), included here because it edits the same call sites as the identity conversion\n- Migrate the DSCI controller's structured field names from the non-standard `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), completing the transition in a single release\n- Handle cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization): emit `name` and `resourceKind`, with `namespace` omitted or set to empty string — never a spurious namespace value\n- Add unit tests asserting field presence and correctness by parsing test logger output, per controller tier and across all 15 action files\n- Log consumer regression validation per the backwards-compatibility NFR: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error; deploy a test build to a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack configured, trigger reconciliation errors, and verify ingestion without configuration changes\n- Coordinate with SRE/Support to identify and update any text-based alert rules that rely on message-string patterns, and document the DSCI key migration in release notes with before/after query examples\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a manager-binary reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) instead, measured by static analysis or CI lint flagging zero remaining `log.Error` calls with identity embedded in message text\n- Given the DSCI controller's reconciliation error logs, when the code is updated, then all structured field names use the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) instead of `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, measured by a unit test asserting the standard field names and a codebase grep confirming no references to the old field names remain\n- Given a service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `resourceKind` as a structured field, measured by a unit test parsing test logger output and asserting `resourceKind` is present with the correct value\n- Given an action-file error log in any of the 4 tiers (8 component, 3 service, 1 DSC, 3 module), when the code is updated, then the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by unit tests asserting all three fields with correct values across all 15 action files\n- Given the module controller reconciles any of its 8 module handlers and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`, measured by unit test\n- Given DSC and component controller reconciliation errors, when logged, then the entries include `resourceKind`, measured by unit test (cross-controller queryability)\n- Given a cluster-scoped resource (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) is reconciled and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` and `namespace` is omitted or empty, measured by unit test confirming no spurious namespace value\n- Given the log format changes, when a test build runs in a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack, then structured fields appear and consumers (Loki, CloudWatch) ingest both formats without configuration changes, and text-based alert rules identified with SRE/Support have been updated\n- The DSCI key-name migration ships within a single release, avoiding long-term inconsistency between old and new field names\n- All changes pass the repository's structured-log CI check once it is available (coordination, not a blocking dependency)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P0] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) — manager-binary portion\n- [P0] DSCI controller migrated from non-standard key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to camelCase convention\n- [P0] All service controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind`\n- [P0] All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (15 action files)\n- [P0] Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` (8 module handlers)\n- [P1] DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` (bundled here — same call sites as the P0 conversion work; deferring it would duplicate edits to identical log calls)\n\n## Risks (from strategy)\n\n- Missed error paths during the audit, especially in the action files — mitigated by grep/static-analysis call-site inventory, a coverage checklist, and the CI lint rule\n- Log consumer regression / broken text-based alerts — mitigated by the dev-cluster validation and SRE/Support coordination above\n- Existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on the old DSCI field names — mitigated by release-notes documentation with before/after query examples and a single-release transition\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":63854,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae39cf41832eed50","agent_id":"a1458638327bcdef4","session_id":"91ced742-efea-4af7-ba7b-29299f310824","uuid":"a96ea4a4-91eb-437a-bd3c-2498b21ec539","timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:54:31.190Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
