{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9d97380d-774a-44ae-acf0-c4137c16c75e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9d55d43db78b647","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-bd6rkjmq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E003.md","content":"# Standardize reconciliation error logs in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic applies the structured logging convention to the cloudmanager binary's multi-cloud infrastructure controllers — AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, and CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine — which handle multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). These controllers are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern and already receive `name` and `namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger; the missing pieces are converting message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs and adding `resourceKind`.\n\nThe cloudmanager binary ships with the rhods-operator in the same repository, but its controllers are disjoint from the manager binary's controllers. It is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes, so its impact scope is narrower than the manager binary's.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in the three cloudmanager controllers' reconciliation error paths, using grep/static analysis, and track coverage in a checklist\n- For each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`)\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager reconciliation error logs\n- Confirm whether any non-standard structured key names exist in cloudmanager error paths (per SME input this is \"if applicable\") and, if found, migrate them to the standard camelCase convention\n- Add unit tests asserting field presence and correctness by parsing test logger output\n- Log consumer regression validation per the backwards-compatibility NFR: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error, with the cloud log consumer stack (e.g., CloudWatch) ingesting both formats without configuration changes; coordinate with SRE/Support on any text-based alert rules\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by a unit test asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output\n- Given a cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses structured key-value pairs instead, measured by static analysis or CI lint flagging zero remaining embedded-identity calls in cloudmanager scope\n- If non-standard key names are found in cloudmanager error paths, they are migrated to the camelCase convention, measured by codebase grep confirming no non-standard keys remain\n- Given the log format changes, when a test build runs with the production cloud log consumer stack, then structured fields appear and consumers ingest both formats without configuration changes, and text-based alert rules identified with SRE/Support have been updated\n- All changes pass the repository's structured-log CI check once it is available (coordination, not a blocking dependency)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P0] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) — cloudmanager-binary portion\n- [P0] All cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields\n\n## Notes\n\n- Release phasing is an open product question (owner: PM / ODH Operator team): this work may ship in the same release as the manager-binary standardization or follow in a subsequent release. This epic is separately scoped so either phasing is possible without affecting the manager-binary work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":66092,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae39cf41832eed50","agent_id":"a1458638327bcdef4","session_id":"91ced742-efea-4af7-ba7b-29299f310824","uuid":"41c662ef-2dbc-4899-9404-84f55086fed7","timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:54:53.237Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
