{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4c64b2ff5904bee","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\tOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage Decision\n15\t\n16\tNot triaged as below-threshold or docs-only. The strategy is S-sized, affects a single component (rhods-operator), and is implemented by a single team (ODH Operator team), but the below-threshold escape condition for multiple distinct work streams applies: the scope spans (1) mechanical code implementation across two binaries, (2) CI enforcement tooling, and (3) substantive content authoring — a new contribution guideline documenting the convention and its publication for external operator adoption (P1 HLR, ~0.5 sprint of the estimate). These are architecturally distinct work products requiring different skills and review cycles, so a full decomposition was produced.\n17\t\n18\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input Overrides\n19\t\n20\tStaff Engineer Input was parsed first and takes precedence; the following overrides are already reflected in the strategy body and are carried into this decomposition:\n21\t\n22\t1. **Action file count corrected**: 15 total action files across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module), not \"~16 component action files\". All epic scope statements use the SME-confirmed counts.\n23\t2. **Module controller tier added**: the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) and its 3 action files are in scope (P0 HLR), not omitted.\n24\t3. **Premise-wrong open question removed**: the question assuming not all 8 components have action files was answered (8/8 confirmed) and does not appear in the open-question evaluation below.\n25\t\n26\t## Component Mappings\n27\t\n28\t- **rhods-operator → \"OpenShift AI Productization\"**: rhods-operator is not a canonical component in `.context/rhai-components.txt`; \"OpenShift AI Productization\" is the closest parent-level component for the RHOAI platform operator. Applies to the investigation, manager, cloudmanager, and CI lint epics.\n29\t- **Contribution guideline documentation → \"Documentation\"**: canonical match for the docs-authoring work product.\n30\t- rhods-operator **is in the platform** (architecture context file exists: `architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`, confirming controller-runtime/logr usage, the cloudmanager binary, and the shared reconciler pattern) — no onboarding chain is needed.\n31\t\n32\t## Epic List\n33\t\n34\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n35\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Resolve structured log field convention scope (fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind) | Investigation | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize reconciliation error logs across the rhods-operator manager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Standardize reconciliation error logs in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | CI lint rule enforcing structured log fields in reconciliation error paths | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E005 | Document and publish the structured log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n41\t\n42\t## Dependency DAG\n43\t\n44\t```mermaid\n45\tgraph TD\n46\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: log field convention scope\"] --> E005[\"E005 Docs: convention guideline + publication\"]\n47\t    E002[\"E002 Implementation: manager binary log standardization\"] --> E005\n48\t    E003[\"E003 Implementation: cloudmanager binary log standardization\"] --> E005\n49\t    E004[\"E004 Implementation: CI lint rule\"] --> E005\n50\t```\n51\t\n52\tRoots (parallel start): E001, E002, E003, E004. Critical path length: 2 (e.g., E002 → E005).\n53\t\n54\t## DAG Justification\n55\t\n56\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n57\t|------|------|-----------|\n58\t| E001 → E005 | Step 5 Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The convention field-set decision (open question: fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind, e.g. reconciliation attempt count, error category) determines the contribution guideline's content. True gate: `rewrite` — the doc must specify additional fields and their semantics if the convention is extended. Recorded via `gated_by` on E005. |\n59\t| E002 → E005 | Step 5 Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementations) | Docs describe what was built; must follow manager implementation. |\n60\t| E003 → E005 | Step 5 Rule 11 | Docs must follow cloudmanager implementation. |\n61\t| E004 → E005 | Step 5 Rule 11 | The guideline references the CI enforcement mechanism; docs follow it. |\n62\t| No E002 ↔ E003 edge | Step 5 Rule 15 (same repo, different areas → parallel) | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries with disjoint controllers; merge conflicts are a coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n63\t| No E004 → E002/E003 edge | Step 5 Rule 15 / Rule 10 analog | The lint rule is developed in parallel; compliance is enforced as an acceptance criterion gate on the implementation epics (\"lint passes once available\"), never by a DAG dependency. |\n64\t| No E001 → E002/E003/E004 edge | Step 3 decision rule | The P0 HLRs fix the required fields (name, namespace, resourceKind); the open question concerns optional extensions only. The implementation epics do the same work regardless of the outcome — any adopted extra fields would be additive follow-up scope, not a change to what these epics build. Hence E001 gates only E005. |\n65\t\n66\t### Open-question evaluation (Step 3)\n67\t\n68\t| Open question | Changes which epics exist / what they do? | Handling |\n69\t|---------------|-------------------------------------------|----------|\n70\t| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (attempt count, error category)? | Yes — changes what the guideline documents (and would add follow-up scope) | Investigation epic E001, gating E005 (`rewrite`) |\n71\t| Which existing log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns? | No — implementation proceeds identically; failure = fix-and-retry | Acceptance criterion on E002/E003 (log consumer regression validation, SRE/Support coordination) |\n72\t| Cloudmanager same release or subsequent release? | No — the epic exists and does the same work either way; only timing changes | E003 is separately scoped so it can follow in a subsequent release; flagged as a health warning for PM |\n73\t| Actual percentage of log calls embedding identity (75% claimed)? | No — the conversion work is identical regardless of the percentage | Acceptance criterion on E002/E003 (grep/static-analysis audit with coverage checklist) |\n74\t\n75\tStrategy prerequisites (\"Pending human review\": architecture review, build onboarding, licence validation, etc.) are process gates on the strategy, not technical unknowns — they do not generate epics and are captured as `human_process_gates = -1` on the implementation epics.\n76\t\n77\t### Epic boundary decisions\n78\t\n79\t- **Manager binary = one epic (E002)**: same component, same team, same repo, one uniform logical change (mechanical conversion to one convention) estimated at ~0.75–1 sprint total. The five controller tiers are not split into separate epics — that would be over-decomposition of a single mechanical pattern.\n80\t- **P1 HLR (resourceKind on DSC/component controllers) stays bundled in E002**: it edits the exact same `log.Error` call sites as the P0 identity-conversion work in those controllers — tightly coupled to the same implementation surface; splitting would duplicate edits and create artificial boundaries (Step 4 bundling rule).\n81\t- **DSCI key-name migration stays bundled in E002**: same controller, same call sites, same PR review cycle as the identity conversion; its distinct risks (single-release transition, query breakage) are captured as acceptance criteria.\n82\t- **Cloudmanager = separate epic (E003)**: distinct binary, disjoint controllers, separately deployable (non-OpenShift clouds only), and a PM open question explicitly contemplates deferring it to a subsequent release.\n83\t- **CI lint rule = separate epic (E004)**: distinct work product (CI/static-analysis tooling vs. Go log edits) with a different review cycle; it is repo-wide and must not be serialized against either binary's implementation.\n84\t- **Docs = separate epic (E005)**: substantive new content (contribution guideline, migration guide, external publication), a different work-product type warranting `docs-authoring` routing. Priority P1 per the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter priority: Major → P1); it does not push priority upstream.\n85\t\n86\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n87\t\n88\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n89\t|-----|----------|---------|\n90\t| All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace converted to structured KV pairs | P0 | E002 (manager), E003 (cloudmanager); enforced/measured by E004 |\n91\t| DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | P0 | E002 |\n92\t| Service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 |\n93\t| All 15 action-file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) | P0 | E002 |\n94\t| Cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E003 |\n95\t| Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E002 |\n96\t| DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (queryability enhancement) | P1 | E002 (bundled — same call sites as P0 work) |\n97\t| Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E005 (gated by E001) |\n98\t\n99\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n100\t\n101\t## Health Warnings\n102\t\n103\t- **Cloudmanager release phasing (open question)**: E003 is P0 per its HLR, but the strategy's open question (owner: PM / ODH Operator team) may defer it to a subsequent release. The epic is separately scoped to permit this; PM should confirm phasing before planning.\n104\t- **Convention extension risk**: if investigation E001 decides to add fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind, the implementation epics (which implement the HLR-mandated fields only) would need follow-up scope for the additional fields. This is accepted to avoid serializing all P0 work behind a product decision.\n105\t- **Unconfirmed audit figure**: the \"75% of log calls embed identity\" figure is SME-estimated and unvalidated; the audit is an acceptance criterion on E002/E003, and the checklist mitigates missed call sites.\n106\t- **Pending strategy process gates**: architecture review, build onboarding, and licence validation are all \"Pending human review\" — these gate strategy execution (reflected in `human_process_gates = -1`) but are not decomposed into epics.\n107\t- **Log consumer regression risk**: text-based alert rules may break when message strings change; captured as acceptance criteria (dev-cluster validation with the production log consumer stack, SRE/Support coordination) rather than a separate epic, per the strategy's own mitigation.\n108\t\n109\t## Tiered Delivery\n110\t\n111\tThe only external dependency with uncertain timing is **COO team adoption** (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator log format changes). Per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI neither mandates nor manages external operator dependencies, and the RFE approval comment confirms voluntary adoption — so no Tier 2 epic is modeled for COO work. All value in this strategy (Tier 1) is delivered independently of COO adoption: the rhods-operator log standardization, CI enforcement, and the published convention. E005 includes filing tracking issues in the COO projects as best-effort outreach; adoption timing is outside this decomposition's control.\n112\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-972a2189833627f2","agent_id":"a71cbd7c9313f2a86","session_id":"91ced742-efea-4af7-ba7b-29299f310824","uuid":"66a3bc7b-4177-4a56-85eb-31aef61cd194","timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:57:38.260Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4c64b2ff5904bee","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
