{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3d500231d3d71d9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n3\ttitle: Standardize reconciliation error logs in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 0\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: -1\n18\t  repo_access: 0\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 4\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Standardize reconciliation error logs in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic applies the structured logging convention to the cloudmanager binary's multi-cloud infrastructure controllers — AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, and CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine — which handle multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). These controllers are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern and already receive `name` and `namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger; the missing pieces are converting message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs and adding `resourceKind`.\n34\t\n35\tThe cloudmanager binary ships with the rhods-operator in the same repository, but its controllers are disjoint from the manager binary's controllers. It is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes, so its impact scope is narrower than the manager binary's.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in the three cloudmanager controllers' reconciliation error paths, using grep/static analysis, and track coverage in a checklist\n40\t- For each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`)\n41\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager reconciliation error logs\n42\t- Confirm whether any non-standard structured key names exist in cloudmanager error paths (per SME input this is \"if applicable\") and, if found, migrate them to the standard camelCase convention\n43\t- Add unit tests asserting field presence and correctness by parsing test logger output\n44\t- Log consumer regression validation per the backwards-compatibility NFR: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error, with the cloud log consumer stack (e.g., CloudWatch) ingesting both formats without configuration changes; coordinate with SRE/Support on any text-based alert rules\n45\t\n46\t## Acceptance Criteria\n47\t\n48\t- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by a unit test asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output\n49\t- Given a cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses structured key-value pairs instead, measured by static analysis or CI lint flagging zero remaining embedded-identity calls in cloudmanager scope\n50\t- If non-standard key names are found in cloudmanager error paths, they are migrated to the camelCase convention, measured by codebase grep confirming no non-standard keys remain\n51\t- Given the log format changes, when a test build runs with the production cloud log consumer stack, then structured fields appear and consumers ingest both formats without configuration changes, and text-based alert rules identified with SRE/Support have been updated\n52\t- All changes pass the repository's structured-log CI check once it is available (coordination, not a blocking dependency)\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability\n55\t\n56\t- [P0] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) — cloudmanager-binary portion\n57\t- [P0] All cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields\n58\t\n59\t## Notes\n60\t\n61\t- Release phasing is an open product question (owner: PM / ODH Operator team): this work may ship in the same release as the manager-binary standardization or follow in a subsequent release. This epic is separately scoped so either phasing is possible without affecting the manager-binary work.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-972a2189833627f2","agent_id":"a71cbd7c9313f2a86","session_id":"91ced742-efea-4af7-ba7b-29299f310824","uuid":"fc27e457-eece-416b-a8cf-38f3d09ab3fa","timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:57:47.367Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3d500231d3d71d9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
