{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4ef00ffaa416bc7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'Scope duplication: E002 and E005 independently author the same DSCI\n10\t    key-migration release-notes documentation with before/after query examples (E002\n11\t    scope bullet; E005 scope bullet and acceptance criterion). The deliverable should\n12\t    have a single owner - E005 as the docs-authoring epic - with E002 referencing\n13\t    it instead of re-authoring it.'\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: HLR Coverage\n16\t  description: The P1 HLR (resourceKind on DSC and component controller error logs)\n17\t    is bundled into P0 epic E002, so it cannot be deferred independently of the P0\n18\t    conversion work. Defensible given the same log.Error call sites, but planners\n19\t    cannot cut the P1 queryability enhancement separately.\n20\terror: null\n21\t---\n22\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8010\n23\t\n24\t## Review Summary\n25\t\n26\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n27\tRecommendation: revise\n28\t\n29\tTriage check: `triage` is null (full decomposition); 5 epics is expected, not flagged.\n30\t\n31\t## Criterion Details\n32\t\n33\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n34\tAll 8 HLRs (6 P0, 2 P1) map to at least one epic, and the traceability matrix in the decomposition summary is complete and matches the epic files' HLR Traceability sections. Priority inheritance is correct: E002/E003/E004 are P0 matching their P0 HLRs; E001 (P1) gates only E005 (P1), no P0 work is blocked by a lower-priority epic; E005 is a docs-authoring epic whose P1 derives from the strategy's Jira priority (Major → P1) per the docs-authoring exemption — its P0 dependencies are not a violation.\n35\t\n36\tMinor (does not reduce score): the P1 HLR \"DSC and component controller error logs include resourceKind\" is bundled into P0 epic E002, so it cannot be deferred independently of the P0 conversion work. The bundling is explicitly justified (same `log.Error` call sites; splitting would duplicate edits) and the P1 addition is close to incidental polish on the same mechanical edit, so this is not scored as priority collapse — but planners should be aware the P1 queryability enhancement ships only with the P0 bundle.\n37\t\n38\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n39\tNo circular dependencies. All four edges (E001→E005, E002→E005, E003→E005, E004→E005) are present in both the mermaid diagram (correct dependency→dependent direction) and E005's frontmatter `dependencies`; the roots E001–E004 have empty dependency lists, matching the diagram. Edges are justified: E002/E003/E004 → E005 are Rule 11 (implementations → docs-authoring) edges, which are valid and do not trigger priority inheritance; E001 → E005 is a legitimate Investigation gate recorded via `gated_by` on E005. Parallelism is handled correctly: manager (E002), cloudmanager (E003), and CI lint (E004) work in different code surfaces with no shared artifact, and the lint-rule coupling to the implementation epics is correctly expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate (\"pass the CI check once available — coordination, not a blocking dependency\"), not a DAG edge. No konflux-onboarding/license-validation/repo-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 pairings do not apply. No branch files exist. Critical path length 2 is within the S-size heuristic (1-2). Cross-checked epic content for uncaptured data/artifact dependencies — none found beyond the edges present.\n40\t\n41\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n42\t**Major — scope duplication across sibling epics:** E002 and E005 independently claim the same work product: DSCI key-migration release-notes documentation with before/after query examples. E002's scope includes \"document the DSCI key migration in release notes with before/after query examples\", and E005's scope includes \"Include the DSCI key-name migration guide: release-notes documentation with before/after query examples for operators who have Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on the old `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` field names\", backed by E005's acceptance criterion \"The DSCI key migration is documented with before/after query examples for existing log queries\". Two epics authoring the same deliverable means the boundary was drawn incorrectly: the migration-guide documentation should be owned by exactly one epic (E005, the docs-authoring epic, is the natural owner; E002 should reference it rather than re-author it), or the epics should be re-split so the documentation has a single owner. This also blurs the work-product type boundary: E002 is a code-implementation epic that bundles content authoring duplicated by the docs epic.\n43\t\n44\tOtherwise boundaries are sound: all epics are ODH Operator team; component/team tuples are respected (code epics on \"OpenShift AI Productization\", docs epic on \"Documentation\"); E002 (~0.75–1 sprint), E003 (~0.25 sprint), E004, E005 are each within epic size; E002/E003/E004/E005 are distinct work products with no other duplication (manager vs. cloudmanager are disjoint controllers; the lint rule is distinct tooling; E005 references rather than re-authors the CI mechanism).\n45\t\n46\tBecause a major issue was found in this criterion, it scores 0 per the scoring rules.\n47\t\n48\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n49\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (whether the convention extends beyond the three required fields) changes what downstream epic E005 documents, and E005 depends on it with `gated_by: E001` and a populated `gate_failure_impact` (action: rewrite, fallback approach specified) — a true gate, not a scheduling dependency. The remaining open questions were correctly evaluated as non-structural (alert-rule inventory and audit percentage become ACs; cloudmanager phasing is timing only). E002/E003/E004 are