{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aee3745ed4ca54b1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E005\n3\ttitle: Document and publish the structured log field convention\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: Documentation\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n14\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E004\n15\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n16\tgate_failure_impact:\n17\t  action: rewrite\n18\t  fallback_approach: If the convention is extended beyond name/namespace/resourceKind,\n19\t    the guideline must be rewritten to specify the additional fields, their semantics,\n20\t    and log-volume implications; implementations of the already-standardized logs\n21\t    would need follow-up work to emit the additional fields.\n22\tai_signals:\n23\t  change_specificity: 1\n24\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n25\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n26\t  existing_foundation: 0\n27\t  open_questions: -1\n28\t  external_dependency: 0\n29\t  human_process_gates: -1\n30\t  repo_access: 1\n31\t  architecture_claims: 1\n32\tinvestigation_signals: null\n33\tai_implementability: Medium\n34\tai_implementability_score: 1\n35\tjira_key: null\n36\tbranch: null\n37\t---\n38\t# Document and publish the structured log field convention\n39\t\n40\t## Description\n41\t\n42\tThis epic authors the contribution guideline that documents the rhods-operator structured log field convention and publishes it for new controller development and for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (the Cluster Observability Operator team's Loki Operator and Tempo Operator, per platform policy overlay 0008 — RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies, and the RFE approval comment confirms the voluntary-adoption approach).\n43\t\n44\tThe guideline documents the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale (alignment with controller-runtime's structured log key conventions), and the three-problem diagnosis that motivated the strategy (message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names, missing `resourceKind`). It is authored after the log standardization work so it describes what was actually built, and after the convention field-scope decision so it specifies the final field set.\n45\t\n46\t## Scope\n47\t\n48\t- Author the contribution guideline topic for new controller development, specifying:\n49\t  - Required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) and their semantics, including cluster-scoped resource handling (`namespace` omitted or empty)\n50\t  - The camelCase naming rationale\n51\t  - The three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing `resourceKind`)\n52\t  - Before/after code examples for converting message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs\n53\t- Include the DSCI key-name migration guide: release-notes documentation with before/after query examples for operators who have Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on the old `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` field names\n54\t- Reflect the resolved convention field-set decision (whether the convention includes any fields beyond the three required ones)\n55\t- Reference the CI enforcement mechanism for the convention\n56\t- Publish the convention and file tracking issues in the external operator (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects for voluntary adoption, accepting that external adoption is best-effort per platform policy\n57\t\n58\t## Acceptance Criteria\n59\t\n60\t- The structured log field convention is documented in a contribution guideline specifying the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis (strategy acceptance criterion)\n61\t- The guideline reflects the resolved field-set decision, including any additional fields adopted with their rationale, or an explicit statement that the convention is limited to the three required fields\n62\t- The documentation is technically reviewed against the implemented behavior of the standardized reconciliation error logs (accuracy validation against the implementation and architecture context)\n63\t- The DSCI key migration is documented with before/after query examples for existing log queries\n64\t- The convention is published and tracking issues are filed in the external operator projects for voluntary adoption\n65\t- If the convention field set is expanded beyond the three required fields by the field-scope decision, the guideline documents the additional fields and their intended use\n66\t\n67\t## HLR Traceability\n68\t\n69\t- [P1] Document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption\n70\t\n71\t## Notes\n72\t\n73\t- Depends on the manager-binary and cloudmanager-binary log standardization and the CI enforcement work being complete (docs describe what was built), and on the convention field-scope decision being resolved (the guideline's field specification is rewritten if the convention is extended beyond the three required fields).\n74\t- External operator adoption itself is out of scope and outside RHOAI's control; this epic's deliverable completes upon publication and filing of tracking issues.\n75\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98bf105a555a0a8e","agent_id":"abd5d2cf9a21bd40f","session_id":"91ced742-efea-4af7-ba7b-29299f310824","uuid":"83b65ab2-a733-4942-8ab8-1b3585581527","timestamp":"2026-10-21T18:03:28.276Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aee3745ed4ca54b1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
